<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Aeneas</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Aeneas is a Trojan hero of Greek and Roman legend, son of the prince Anchises and the goddess Venus (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aphrodite">Aphrodite</a>). Roman tradition, given its classic form in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/virgil">Virgil</a>'s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aeneid">Aeneid</a>, told how he escaped the fall of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/troy">Troy</a> and settled in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latium">Latium</a>, founding <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lavinium">Lavinium</a>, while his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ascanius">Ascanius</a> founded Alba Longa. Through this line the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a>, and especially the Julian family, claimed Trojan descent.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/aeneas.jpg" alt="Aeneas" width="320" height="387" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author. · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Iapyx_removing_arrowhead_from_Aeneas.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>In Greek and Roman mythology Aeneas was the son of the Trojan prince Anchises and the goddess <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aphrodite">Aphrodite</a>, known to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> as Venus. Anchises was a first cousin of King Priam of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/troy">Troy</a>, which made Aeneas a second cousin to Priam's children, among them Hector and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>. The Homeric Hymn to Aphrodite tells how <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zeus">Zeus</a> put desire for the mortal Anchises into Aphrodite, who bore Aeneas near Mount Ida and gave him to the nymphs of the mountain to raise.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/homer">Homer</a>'s Iliad Aeneas is a minor character, the leader of the Trojans' Dardanian allies and a principal lieutenant of Hector. The gods twice save him from death, as if for a destiny not yet known. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aphrodite">Aphrodite</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apollo">Apollo</a> rescue him from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diomedes">Diomedes</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poseidon">Poseidon</a> saves him from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/achilles">Achilles</a>, noting that though from a junior branch of the royal family, he is destined to become king of the Trojan people.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/virgil">Virgil</a>'s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aeneid">Aeneid</a> tells the Roman form of the story. Aeneas is one of the few Trojans not killed or enslaved when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/troy">Troy</a> fell. Commanded by the gods to flee, he gathered the group known as the Aeneads, among them his father and his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ascanius">Ascanius</a>, and carried the household gods of Troy to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>. Anchises died at Drepanum in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a>. A storm brought the fleet to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthage</a>, where Aeneas had an affair with its queen, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dido">Dido</a>. Mercury was sent to remind him of his mission and he left in secret. Dido cursed him, a curse said to set Carthage against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/punic-wars">Punic Wars</a>, and killed herself.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In Latium King Latinus welcomed the exiles. Turnus, king of the Rutuli, to whom Latinus's daughter Lavinia had been promised, made war on Aeneas at the urging of Juno and was killed, and there <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/virgil">Virgil</a>'s account ends. According to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/livy">Livy</a>, Aeneas founded the city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lavinium">Lavinium</a>, named after his wife, and after his death Venus asked Jupiter to make him immortal, and he was recognised as the god Jupiter Indiges.</p><p>His son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ascanius">Ascanius</a>, also called Iulus, founded Alba Longa. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aeneid">Aeneid</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romulus">Romulus</a> and Remus descend from Aeneas through their mother Rhea Silvia, and the Julian family of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, among them Julius Cæsar and Augustus, traced its lineage to him and so to Venus.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Aeneas” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In legend the Trojan <b>Aeneas</b>, son of Venus, laid the basis of Roman religion when he brought the Palladium, the Lares and the Penates from Troy, and the Greek exile Evander set up the Greatest Altar of Hercules.</blockquote><a href="/religion/roman-religion">Roman religion · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>The story of <b>Aeneas</b> reaching Italy predates Virgil by centuries.</blockquote><a href="/work/aeneid">Aeneid · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>Roman tradition held that Ascanius, son of the Trojan hero <b>Aeneas</b>, founded Alba Longa about thirty years after Lavinium, and placed its foundation in the 12th century BCE.</blockquote><a href="/state/alba-longa">Alba Longa</a></li></ul></section>
